# Story: a newcomer who only has the keyboard and the mouse, no terminal. Every key that seems dead says why, the
# moments that frighten are explained a few times and then left alone, and an expert's routine stays silent.
# Reference: A65 A66 (F43). `press` runs a command as its shortcut would; window ids start at 101.
# The three tiling switches of the guide's section 2 are off: with macOS's own tiling on, the left, right and top edges of the screen are macOS's (Q23).
mac-tiling off
open Safari
open Mail
open Notes
# The first key they try does nothing, and says what to do instead.
press in
expect fail
expect said Nothing is docked yet: hold ⌘ and drag a window to a screen edge, or try ⌃⌥←
# They drag a window to the left edge. No command ran, and the moment is explained all the same.
drag 101 3 400 cmd
expect docked 101
expect said Docked · ⌃⌥↩ frames it
flush
# They press those keys. Safari fills the screen. Mail and Notes float, so they are where they were, behind it: nothing has
# gone out of view, and Arbor does not say that anything has.
focus 101
press zoom
expect fills 101
expect still 102
expect visible 102
expect visible 103
expect silent
press show-all
# Mail and Notes go to the right edge, and they frame Safari again. Now the two vanish: the sentence that keeps a newcomer.
drag 102 1437 400
expect docked 102
drag 103 1437 400
expect docked 103
focus 101
press zoom
expect fills 101
expect hidden 102
expect hidden 103
expect said Your other windows are out of view, not closed · ⌃⌥Space shows everything
press show-all
expect visible 102
# The line goes by itself.
flush
expect silent
# Something that cannot be seen is confirmed.
press save-view 1
expect said Saved view 1: everything
flush
# A view that was never saved: the sentence names the keys that would save it.
press view 7
expect fail
expect said View 7 is not saved; “Save View 7” saves where you are looking
flush
# The same commands from a script are answered in the reply only.
view 7
expect fail
expect silent
# After three times the hint is taken as read, and what can be seen for oneself is not announced.
press zoom
press show-all
press zoom
expect said out of view
press show-all
flush
press zoom
expect fills 101
expect silent
press show-all
expect ok
expect silent
# The shortcuts, on screen; any command puts them away.
press show-shortcuts
expect ok
expect silent
press out
expect fail
expect said Already showing everything
# Pausing clears the screen, then says whose the keys are now and how to come back.
press toggle-pause
expect said Arbor is paused: its keys are the apps’ again · ⌃⌥⎋ resumes
flush
expect silent
press toggle-pause
expect said Resumed
# A name shows nowhere on the space, so giving one and taking it off are confirmed. The field is its own answer.
press name Work
expect said Named everything: Work
flush
press ask-name
expect ok
expect silent
field-cancel
press unname
expect said Removed the name Work from everything
# What a command took along is said, with the way back, and the way back works.
focus 101
press zoom
press save-view 3
press show-all
flush
press arrange
expect said 2 saved views are gone · ⌃⌥Z takes it back
press undo
press view 3
expect fills 101
